CREATED:20261014T124543Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261014T124543Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_316692905@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261031T052746Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261128T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261128T140000
SUMMARY:Christmas in Old York! 1833 (RETURN FOR 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\n**If you plan 
 on attending\, please get your tickets for this event early as this tour h
 as run for the last 3 years\, and does sell out rather quickly.**\n\nSo wh
 at was it like in York in 1833 at Christmas time?\n\nThe prices of various
  food items and gifts on display\, the gas lights creating a glow as the s
 now begins\, places to gather\, places to get a drink\, like the Steamboat
  Hotel on Front Street\, or maybe one might decide to take their best girl
  or guy on a stagecoach trip to High Park to watch kids speed down the hil
 ls on their skids.\n\nWhat were the traditions in old York? Maybe the resi
 dents went down to the lake and watched the nighttime fishermen spear fish
  for Christmas dinner. Let's take a trip around old York and see what it w
 as like back in 1833\, three months before we changed our name to Toronto 
 in 1834.\n\nOr take a holiday trip to Kingston or Montreal to buy gifts di
 rectly from New York or London stores\, fancy hats for the ladies and ciga
 rs for the men. Books and wooden toys for the kids. What about that goose 
 in the little grocery store on King Street West of Jarvis? Candies from Br
 itain\, real home made gingerbread in the store windows.\n\nOn Christmas E
 ve everyone walked to St. James Cathedral for the ringing of the bells\, s
 inging\, and skating over the frozen Toronto Bay to the Peninsula.\n\nThis
  tour explores old York. This walking tour is for all walking levels. The 
 tour begins and ends at St. James Cathedral on King Street East at Church 
 Street. A great way to kick off the season.\n\nThis tour has a ticketed pr
 ice of $15.00.\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amoun
 t when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the followi
 ng email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/316692
 905/
