BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_316868918@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261031T052746Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261206T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261206T140000
SUMMARY:Christmas in Old Toronto! 1855 - The Railway Era (NEW FOR 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\n**So what was 
 it like in Toronto in 1855 at Christmas time? This tour is different from 
 the first part - Christmas in 1833 York. If you can\, try and take both to
 urs\, and compare the differences in 22 years of history! Toronto changed 
 significantly between those years.**\n\nImagine spending Christmas in Toro
 nto in 1855\, when the city was still a relatively young place and the str
 eets were a far cry from the Toronto we know today. There would be no elec
 tric Christmas lights\, no department-store displays\, no traffic jams of 
 cars on the DVP. Instead\, Toronto was a city of horse-drawn wagons\, mudd
 y streets\, wood fires\, gas lamps\, small shops and busy markets. And the
  railways were on the way to Toronto.\n\nChristmas was becoming increasing
 ly popular as a family celebration\, influenced by traditions coming from 
 Britain\, Ireland\, Europe and the United States. For many\, Christmas Day
  meant attending church\, visiting relatives and friends\, enjoying a spec
 ial meal and taking a welcome break from everyday life\, even if just for 
 a day.\n\nFor children\, Christmas could be magical\, but it was a much si
 mpler Christmas. Toys were homemade or relatively inexpensive\, and a chil
 d's Christmas might include a wooden toy\, a doll\, some sweets\, an orang
 e\, nuts or perhaps a book. There were no shopping malls or endless advert
 isements telling people what they needed to buy.\n\nToronto's shops and ma
 rkets would nevertheless have been busy in the days before Christmas. The 
 smell of roasting meat\, baked goods\, spices and coal fires would have fi
 lled the air\, while church bells and carriages would have been part of th
 e Christmas landscape. And for those who were poor\, Christmas could be a 
 very different experience\, making charitable giving and church relief par
 ticularly important.\n\nBut perhaps the most fascinating thing about Chris
 tmas in 1855 is how close the holiday still was to the realities of ninete
 enth-century life. Toronto was a city of immigrants\, workers\, merchants\
 , labourers\, professionals and families living in very different circumst
 ances. Some homes would have been warm and comfortable\, while others woul
 d have been cold\, and crowded. There were no modern hospitals\, no antibi
 otics\, no central heating and no easy way of escaping a harsh winter. Yet
  people celebrated\, rich or poor.\n\nThey gathered around fireplaces\, we
 nt to church\, shared food\, told stories and tried to make Christmas spec
 ial. So when we walk through Toronto and imagine Christmas in 1855\, we're
  not simply looking at an old-fashioned Christmas\, we're stepping into a 
 city where Christmas was beginning to take on many of the traditions we re
 cognize today\, but where life itself was more uncertain.\n\nThis tour exp
 lores old Toronto during the Christmas season of 1855. This walking tour i
 s for all walking levels. The tour begins and ends at St. James Cathedral 
 on King Street East at Church Street. A great way to kick off the season.\
 n\nThis tour has a ticketed price of $15.00.\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Plea
 se e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! You
  can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.
 com**
